[138.765522, "o", "[approval] bash: cd /app && F='[.[]|select(.status==\"active\")|{user_id:.id,username:.username,email:.email,last_login:(.last_login|split(\"T\")[0]),role_count:(.roles|length),primary_role:(.roles[0]//null)}]|sort_by(.username)'\r\nesc=$(printf '%s' \"$F\" | sed 's/\"/\\\\\"/g')\r\nprintf 'filter: \"%s\"\\nargs:\\n  - \".\"\\n' \"$esc\" > /tmp/optA.yaml\r\nprintf 'filter: \"%s\"\\nargs:\\n  - \"%s\"\\n' \"$esc\" \"$esc\" > /tmp/optB.yaml\r\nfor opt in A B; do\r\n  rm -f /tmp/out.json\r\n  jq $(yq -r '.args[]' /tmp/opt$opt.yaml) \"$(yq -r '.filter' /tmp/opt$opt.yaml)\" /app/users.json > /tmp/out.json 2>/tmp/e_$opt\r\n  echo \"opt$opt: exit=$? stderr_bytes=$(wc -c </tmp/e_$opt) identical=$(cmp -s /tmp/ref.json /tmp/out.json && echo yes || echo no)\"\r\ndone — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /app && F='[.[]|select(.status==\"active\")|{user_id:.id,username:.us…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.8-flash-next — 37 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: cd /app && rm -f /app/active_users_report.json && sh /app/cmd.sh; echo \"cmd_exit=$?\"; jq -e 'type==\"array\" and lengt"]
[138.765553, "o", "h==346 and all(.last_login|test(\"^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$\")) and ([.[].username]==([.[].username]|sort))' /app/active_users_report.json >/dev/null && echo FINAL_OK; jq '.[0], (.[]|select(.role_count==0)|.username)' /app/active_users_report.json | head -12 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /app && rm -f /app/active_users_report.json && sh /app/cmd.sh; echo…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.8-flash-next — 38 tool call(s) so far\r\n  qwen3.8-flash-next — 39 tool call(s) so far\r\n  ⚙ note \r\n    ✓ note — In this container `yq` is the Python yq wrapper around jq (r\r\nDone. All three artifacts are on disk and verified.\r\n\r\n**What was produced**\r\n\r\n- `/app/active_users_report.json` — 346 objects (from 500 input users; statuses present were `active`/`inactive`/`pending`, only `active` kept), 2-space indented, sorted ascending by `username`, with `user_id`, `username`, `email`, `last_login` (`YYYY-MM-DD`), `role_count`, and `primary_role` (`null` for the 50 active users with an empty `roles` array).\r\n- `/app/cm"]
[138.765568, "o", "d.sh` — the exact jq command with redirection:\r\n  `jq '[.[]|select(.status==\"active\")|{user_id:.id,username:.username,email:.email,last_login:(.last_login|split(\"T\")[0]),role_count:(.roles|length),primary_role:(.roles[0]//null)}]|sort_by(.username)' /app/users.json > /app/active_users_report.json`\r\n- `/app/jq_args.yaml` — `filter` plus a single `args` entry carrying the same filter, so the documented reconstruction command supplies jq exactly one filter positional.\r\n\r\n**Verification**\r\n\r\n- `sh /app/cmd.sh` regenerates the report byte-identical to the reference output (`cmp` clean, exit 0).\r\n- The documented reconstruction `jq $(yq -r '.args[]' /app/jq_args.yaml) \"$(yq -r '.filter' /app/jq_args.yaml)\" /app/users.json > ...` also produces a byte-identical file.\r\n- Structural assertions all pass: array of 346, all six keys present on every object, every `last_login` matches `^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$`, usernames sorted, and `primary_role` is `null` exactly when `role_count == 0`.\r\n\r\n**Two constraints wort"]
[138.765574, "o", "h knowing**\r\n\r\n- `yq` here is the Python yq wrapping jq; it rejects an unquoted YAML scalar beginning with `[`, so the filter is stored as a double-quoted scalar with `\"` escaped as `\\\"`.\r\n- Because the reconstruction command expands `$(yq -r '.args[]')` unquoted, any space inside an argument splits it and corrupts the filter. The filter is therefore written space-free (`.roles[0]//null` instead of an `if/then/else/end` block, no spaces after `:` or `,`). As a side effect jq prints one harmless `Could not open file …` line to stderr during the reconstruction (the duplicated positional is treated as a filename) while still writing correct output; running `/app/cmd.sh` directly is clean.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=qwen3.8-flash-next tools=39 surface=b1d6c95da52f\r\n"]
